  "grammar": {
    "title": "Using 'Ser' for Occupations",
    "explanation": "In Spanish, you use the verb 'ser' (to be) to talk about occupations. Unlike English, you typically do NOT use an article (a/an) before the profession:\n\n• Yo soy profesor. - I am a teacher.\n• Ella es médica. - She is a doctor.\n• Ellos son ingenieros. - They are engineers.\n\nHowever, if you add an adjective, you include the article: 'Ella es una buena médica.' (She is a good doctor.)\n\nMany professions have masculine and feminine forms: profesor/profesora, médico/médica, abogado/abogada.",
    "examples": [
      { "target": "Soy estudiante de la universidad.", "native": "I am a university student." },
      { "target": "Mi hermana es enfermera.", "native": "My sister is a nurse." },
      { "target": "Ellos son buenos cocineros.", "native": "They are good cooks." }
    ]
  },
